## Mode A (measure on the old environment): Capture Procedure

### 1. Old environment information

| Item | Value |
|------|---|
| Host | <OLD_HOST> |
| OS | <SOURCE_OS_VERSION> |
| App version | <APP_VERSION> |
| Dependent libraries | <LIBS_AND_VERSIONS> |
| Capture date | YYYY-MM-DD |

### 2. Example capture commands

```bash
# --- HTTP response ---
curl -v http://<OLD_HOST>:<PORT>/ > baseline/http-response.txt 2>&1

# --- HTTPS response (including TLS handshake) ---
curl -vk https://<OLD_HOST>:<HTTPS_PORT>/ > baseline/https-response.txt 2>&1

# --- Header check ---
curl -I http://<OLD_HOST>:<PORT>/<PATH> > baseline/headers.txt 2>&1

# --- Error log (pattern under normal operation) ---
cp <ERROR_LOG_PATH> baseline/error-log-normal.txt

# --- syscall trace (for one normal request) ---
strace -T -e trace=network -p <PID> -o baseline/strace-normal.txt &
curl -s http://<OLD_HOST>:<PORT>/ > /dev/null
kill %1

# --- Performance baseline ---
for i in $(seq 1 10); do
  curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{time_total}\n' http://<OLD_HOST>:<PORT>/
done > baseline/response-times.txt

# --- Fixing CLI input/output (Characterization) ---
# Save the full output for a representative set of inputs
<CLI_BIN> <ARGS_1> > baseline/cli-case1.txt 2>&1
<CLI_BIN> <ARGS_2> > baseline/cli-case2.txt 2>&1
```

### 3. Storage location

```
02-test/test-results/baseline/
├── <observation-file>.txt    # Output of the commands above
└── known-diffs.md            # Record of intentional diffs (append after migration)
```

### 4. Post-migration comparison

```bash
# After migration, capture with the same commands and diff (or make -f verify.sh diff-baseline)
diff baseline/http-response.txt current/http-response.txt
```

---

## Mode B (define by reading the code): Definition Procedure (no old environment)

### 1. Defining expected behavior

Describe each component in the following format. **The evidence column is required**:

| # | Component | Observation point | Expected behavior | Evidence (source:line / constant) | Confidence |
|---|--------------|--------|---------|------------------------|------|
| B-1 | <component> | Startup | <what happens at startup> | <file.c:123> | High/Medium/Low |
| B-2 | <component> | Core | <input/output of the main functionality> | <file.c:456> | High/Medium/Low |
| B-3 | <component> | IPC/External | <externally observable state: port, property, file> | <file.c:789> | High/Medium/Low |
| B-4 | <component> | Exit | <conditions for normal/abnormal termination and exit codes> | <file.c:012> | High/Medium/Low |

### 2. Conversion into Testable Assertions

Convert each expected behavior into a "command that can be run and observed after migration + expected result," and
register it in a script under `02-test/integration/` or in verify-smoke.
**Any expected behavior that cannot be converted must be recorded as "unverifiable" in the risk section of the work-plan.**

### 3. Updating the baseline

If an error in the baseline description is discovered during implementation, correct it on the spot and update the "confidence."
Since the correction history can be tracked via git, overwriting is fine (but write the reason in the commit message).

---

## Notes (common to both modes)

- Normalize dynamic values such as timestamps and PIDs before comparison (e.g., strip them with sed)
- Allow performance variation of roughly ±20% as acceptable
- The problem is not "that a diff exists" but "that an unintended diff exists"
- Record intentional diffs (e.g., a version change in the Server header) in `known-diffs.md`
